@Don H

"Go AWAY" is an imperative sentence (command) consisting of a verb and an adverb.  The subject is the implied second person "you," which is not usually stated in an imperative sentence.

"Come here" works the same way.

@lucky13
Ni.
Adverbs of place describe where an action happens, answering the question "where?". They usually come after the main verb or the sentence's object. Common examples include words denoting position (here, there), direction (upstairs, away), or unspecified locations (everywhere, somewhere).

@lucky13

AWAY and here modify the verbs "go" and "come" and indicate direction.  Movement to a point more distant to the speaker, and movement to a point closer to the speaker.

As Dan points out, "Where?" is an adverb; AWAY and here serve the same function as "where?" in the answer.

